e polygon includes the origin as a vertex, and the other vertices are on rays. The polygon goes from origin to first point, then to second, ..., to last, then back to origin. That is exactly a simple polygon (a convex polygon if the points are in convex position? Not necessarily convex, but it is simple because the edges are between consecutive rays and the origin. However, there could be self-intersections if the radii are not monotonic? But here radii alternate, so they are not monotonic: a, b, a, b,... So the distances go up and down. But the polygon edges are between points on consecutive rays. Since the rays are in order, the edge between a point on ray i and a point on ray i+1 lies entirely within the sector between those rays (if the radii are positive). That edge will not cross other edges because other edges lie in different sectors. The only potential crossing is with the edge from the last point back to the origin. That edge goes from ray 9θ to origin, crossing through all sectors? Actually the edge from A11 (angle 9θ, distance b) to A1 (origin) is a straight line along the ray at angle 9θ? No, it's from the point to the origin, so it lies along the ray at angle 9θ (since it goes from origin to that point). So it lies exactly on the ray at angle 9θ. That ray is the last ray. The other edges are between rays at angles iθ and (i+1)θ. They lie within the sector between those rays. The edge from origin to A11 lies on the boundary of the sector between 9θ and 0? Actually careful: The rays are at angles 0, θ, 2θ, ..., 9θ. The edge from A11 to A1 goes from angle 9θ to origin, so it lies along the ray at 9θ. That ray is also the boundary of the sector between 8θ and 9θ? Actually the edge from A10 to A11 lies between rays 8θ and 9θ. The edge from A11 to A1 lies exactly on ray 9θ. So it might touch the edge from A10 to A11 at the endpoint A11, but not cross. So the polygon is simple. Also the interior angle at A1 is the angle between the edge from A11 to A1 (which 